WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.016 --> 00:00:19.646
When regained consciousness, Sherlock Holmes was bending over me with a glass of water.

2
00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:24.408
"My dear Watson, I owe you a thousand apologies," said the familiar voice.

3
00:00:24.428 --> 00:00:27.891
"I had no idea you would be so shaken by my reappearance."

4
00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:29.972
"Holmes, is it really you?"

5
00:00:30.644 --> 00:00:34.567
I gripped his lean, powerful arms and knew that this wasn't a ghost or a dream.

6
00:00:34.587 --> 00:00:37.910
"How did you manage to emerge alive from the Reichenbach Falls?"

7
00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.572
"Are you sure you're well enough to hear the story?"

8
00:00:41.372 --> 00:00:43.194
Holmes peered at me with a look of concern.

9
00:00:43.214 --> 00:00:46.576
"My dramatic reappearance has given you a serious shock."

10
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:49.358
"I'm all right, but I can hardly believe my eyes."

11
00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:51.780
I rubbed them and blinked a few times.

12
00:00:52.581 --> 00:00:56.304
We sat down, and I saw that Holmes was still wearing the shabby coat of the bookseller.

13
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.462
The rest of that individual lay on the table in a pile of white hair and old books.

14
00:01:02.382 --> 00:01:07.264
Holmes' thin face looked slightly gray, leading me to conclude that his recent life had not been a

15
00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:07.784
healthy one.

16
00:01:07.804 --> 00:01:10.445
"I'm overjoyed that you're here!"

17
00:01:10.985 --> 00:01:11.366
I said.

18
00:01:11.386 --> 00:01:14.667
"But how did you manage to climb out of that dreadful chasm?"

19
00:01:14.687 --> 00:01:19.429
"I had no serious difficulty getting out of the falls, for the simple reason that I was never

20
00:01:19.529 --> 00:01:19.869
in them."

21
00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:21.890
"You were never in the falls?

22
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.671
But the note you left for me made it sound like you were going to die."

23
00:01:26.332 --> 00:01:26.912
"That's true."

24
00:01:27.776 --> 00:01:28.396
Holmes nodded.

25
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:34.260
"As soon as I saw Moriarty's sinister figure appear on that narrow path, I genuinely thought my life

26
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:34.880
was at an end.

27
00:01:36.881 --> 00:01:40.563
Moriarty had a desperate look in his eyes, as if he knew his time was up and he

28
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.284
only wanted to get his revenge on me."

29
00:01:42.965 --> 00:01:47.527
The two men had exchanged a few words, and Moriarty allowed Holmes to write that final note.

30
00:01:48.528 --> 00:01:53.431
After leaving it under the stone, Holmes continued to walk along the path with Moriarty at his heels.

31
00:01:53.451 --> 00:01:58.698
"When I reached the end of the path, Moriarty suddenly rushed at me and threw his long arms

32
00:01:58.738 --> 00:02:00.179
around me," Holmes said.

33
00:02:00.199 --> 00:02:05.122
"We tottered on the edge of the falls, both of us about to go into the chasm.

34
00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:09.625
At the last second, I managed to slip from his grip and watched as he fell to his

35
00:02:09.645 --> 00:02:10.386
death in the falls."

36
00:02:12.527 --> 00:02:16.470
Both of us were silent for a moment, contemplating how close Holmes had come to death.

37
00:02:17.391 --> 00:02:22.014
I was amazed and grateful that Holmes had managed to defeat Moriarty, but I was still puzzled.

38
00:02:22.034 --> 00:02:24.175
"How do you explain the tracks?"

39
00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:25.116
I asked.

40
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:30.053
"With my own eyes, I saw that two sets of footprints went to the end of the path

41
00:02:30.153 --> 00:02:31.053
and none returned."

42
00:02:31.073 --> 00:02:35.935
"I immediately realized that it would be to my advantage if everyone thought I was dead."

43
00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:41.696
Holmes reminded me that Inspector Lestrade had failed to capture the most dangerous members of Moriarty's gang.

44
00:02:42.677 --> 00:02:46.718
"Moriarty's death would only increase their desire for revenge," Holmes explained.

45
00:02:47.718 --> 00:02:50.799
"If they knew I was alive, one of them would certainly get me.

46
00:02:52.619 --> 00:02:55.060
But if they thought I was dead, I could keep my eyes on them.

47
00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.901
Sooner or later they would make themselves vulnerable, and I could either destroy them or have them arrested."

48
00:03:01.982 --> 00:03:05.243
So, with some difficulty, Holmes began to climb the cliff above the path.

49
00:03:06.163 --> 00:03:08.583
Partway up he reached a ledge, where he could lie hidden.

50
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:13.305
"I watched you and the Swiss police investigate my death in a completely inefficient manner.

51
00:03:14.105 --> 00:03:18.906
When you had formed your inevitable—but wrong—conclusions, you all departed for the hotel."

52
00:03:19.686 --> 00:03:20.887
I frowned at his criticism.

53
00:03:20.907 --> 00:03:22.787
"We did the best we could.

54
00:03:23.707 --> 00:03:29.121
I was distraught at your death, and the police—" "At any rate, I was left alone," Holmes interrupted.

55
00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:32.762
"I thought I'd reached the end of my adventures for that day, but I was wrong."

56
00:03:34.742 --> 00:03:38.644
Suddenly a huge rock fell from above, striking the path below and bouncing into the chasm.

57
00:03:39.604 --> 00:03:41.484
At first Holmes thought it was an accident.

58
00:03:42.345 --> 00:03:45.406
But looking up, he saw a man peering over the edge of the cliff.

59
00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.566
Another rock struck within a foot of Holmes' head.

60
00:03:48.586 --> 00:03:53.828
"An accomplice of Moriarty's had stood guard while the professor attacked me," Holmes said.

61
00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.802
"He had seen everything and was now attempting to finish the job Moriarty had started."

62
00:03:59.682 --> 00:04:03.784
When Holmes saw the man looking over the edge of the cliff again, he decided to scramble down

63
00:04:03.844 --> 00:04:04.364
to the path.

64
00:04:05.424 --> 00:04:09.246
Another rock sailed past him as he hung by his hands from the edge of the ledge.

65
00:04:10.126 --> 00:04:12.807
Partway down he slipped and landed bleeding on the path.

66
00:04:14.908 --> 00:04:18.110
That night Holmes walked ten miles over the hills in the darkness.

67
00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.370
A week later he was in Italy.

68
00:04:20.391 --> 00:04:22.852
"But who was that man watching you?"

69
00:04:23.312 --> 00:04:23.752
I asked.

70
00:04:24.628 --> 00:04:27.891
"All I knew at the time was that he was dangerous," Holmes said.

71
00:04:27.911 --> 00:04:33.936
"I've spent the last three years traveling the world, trying simultaneously to evade and capture the last of

72
00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:34.716
Moriarty's men.

73
00:04:35.617 --> 00:04:39.981
I had only one confidant, my brother, Mycroft, whom I needed to supply me with funds."

74
00:04:40.001 --> 00:04:42.703
"You could have trusted me to keep your secret."

75
00:04:42.723 --> 00:04:47.167
"I apologize, my dear Watson, but it was vital that you should think me dead.

76
00:04:47.907 --> 00:04:50.009
Several times I picked up my pen to write to you.

77
00:04:50.629 --> 00:04:53.532
But I never wrote for fear that you would inadvertently betray me.

78
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.441
Besides Holmes smiled wryly.

79
00:04:57.461 --> 00:05:02.082
"Could you have written such a convincing account of my death if you knew I was still alive?"

80
00:05:03.043 --> 00:05:05.143
I grudgingly agreed that he was probably right.

81
00:05:05.163 --> 00:05:09.324
"That's why I hurried away from you this evening," Holmes explained gently.

82
00:05:09.344 --> 00:05:11.465
"I'm in the midst of a dangerous case.

83
00:05:12.365 --> 00:05:16.206
I feared that any show of surprise or emotion from you would draw attention to me."

84
00:05:16.226 --> 00:05:19.847
"And it's this case that has brought you back to London?"

85
00:05:20.307 --> 00:05:20.728
I asked.

86
00:05:20.748 --> 00:05:25.162
"Yes, I also learned recently that only one of my enemies remains in London.

87
00:05:26.062 --> 00:05:28.684
I decided to return when I read about the Ronald Adair murder.

88
00:05:29.544 --> 00:05:32.306
Something about it reminded me of one of Moriarty's men."

89
00:05:33.147 --> 00:05:37.009
So Holmes returned to his Baker Street rooms, which Mycroft had preserved for him.

90
00:05:37.029 --> 00:05:40.451
"I'm sorry to say I frightened Mrs.

91
00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:42.552
Hudson, but I think our landlady will recover.

92
00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:46.655
This afternoon I found myself in my old armchair in my old rooms."

93
00:05:47.735 --> 00:05:48.596
Holmes smiled at me.

94
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.902
"All that was missing was my friend Watson, sitting in the other chair."

95
00:05:54.043 --> 00:05:57.505
I was grateful for his mention of our friendship and his sympathy for my wife's death.

96
00:05:57.525 --> 00:06:04.150
"Work is the best antidote for sorrow," he said, "and I have a hard and dangerous night's work

97
00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:04.690
for us both."

98
00:06:05.991 --> 00:06:09.874
I begged him to tell more, but he put me off, saying that we had three years of

99
00:06:09.914 --> 00:06:11.435
the past to discuss over supper.

100
00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:13.016
Holmes smiled.

101
00:06:13.757 --> 00:06:16.018
"That's enough to keep us occupied until nine-thirty."

102
00:06:16.999 --> 00:06:17.739
"What happens then?"

103
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:18.600
I asked.

104
00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:22.497
"We embark upon the remarkable adventure of the empty house!"
